# Architecture and patterns

## Routing
- All HTTP/WebSocket entry points implement `BaseRouter` and expose `fun register(route: Route)`.
- Feature routers (e.g. `UserFeatureRouter`) compose sub-routers (auth, user, session, security).
- In host apps, register routers inside `routing { }` (sample is the reference).

## Server bootstrap
- Prefer `KtorServer.create(commonConfig) { module(applicationModule) }`.
- The host application provides the application module (e.g. sample’s `Application.module(appComponent)`).

## Configuration
- Env-based config keys live in `*EnvKeys`.
- Config is built by `*ConfigFactory` and bound via Dagger modules.
- Host app must provide `AppInfo` (name, version); optional holders can supply resolvers/parsers/config.

## Layering
- Routes → use cases → managers/repositories.
- Prefer use-case classes for business steps; managers for orchestration/shared logic.

## Errors
- Centralize error mapping via `AppErrorParser` + status pages.
- Map domain errors to HTTP consistently; keep logs and public messages separated.

## Naming / packaging
- Package by layer/feature: `route`, `usecase`, `manager`, `database`/`repository`, `di/module`, `config`, `security`, etc.
